 (+0.64), which castles: the king gets out of the centre and the rook joins the game."],"better":"18…O-O castles the king to safety. After 19.Nd5 Nc6 20.Nc7 Qf4 21.Rxa7 Rxa7 22.Nxb5 Ra2, Black comes out a pawn up (+0.64)."},{"ply":37,"name":"19.Ra5","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"19.Ra5 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 66% to 49%.","points":["Black answers 19…Bb8: moving away uncovers the rook on a8, which now hits the rook on a5.","Better was 19.Nd5 (+1.81): moving away uncovers the bishop on b2, which now hits the knight on d4."],"better":"19.Nd5 threatens Nc7+, winning a rook and a knight, picking it up with 20.Nxa8. After 19…exd5 20.Rxa7 Rxa7 21.Bxd4 Ra6 22.exd5+ Kd8, White is a pawn down (+1.81)."},{"ply":56,"name":"28…Rfe8","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"28…Rfe8 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 71% to 60%.","points":["White answers 29.Rd1: shores up the d5 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","Better was 28…Qd6 (−2.40): adds a decisive attacker on the d5 pawn: 2 attackers against 1 defender."],"better":"28…Qd6 wins a pawn, picking it up with 29…Qxd5, and threatens …Qxg3+, winning two pawns. After 29.Nd2 Qxd5 30.Qxd5 Bxd5 31.Re5 Be6 32.Rxh5 Rfc8, the queens come off (−2.40)."},{"ply":62,"name":"31…Bd4","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"31…Bd4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moves the attacked bishop to safety.","Protects the queen on f6, which was hanging.","The next best move, 31…Qd6, would drop Black's winning chances from 69% to 54% (−0.45).","31…Bd4 gets the bishop out of the way of Nxc5 and threatens …Qf3, winning the queen for a bishop, picking it up with 33…Bxf2+.","White was threatening Nxc5, winning a bishop and a bishop. The bishop steps away.","It threatens …Qf3, winning the queen for a bishop, picking it up with 33…Bxf2+, so White has to spend the next move on it."]},{"ply":80,"name":"40…Rc1","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"40…Rc1! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the queen.","The rook takes the open c-file.","The next best move, 40…Qc1, would drop Black's winning chances from 82% to 70% (−2.35).","40…Rc1 threatens …Rxc2, winning the queen for two pawns, and stops Nxg6.","It threatens …Rxc2, winning the queen for two pawns, so White has to spend the next move on it.","White was threatening Nxg6, winning a pawn. Rc1 takes care of it."]},{"ply":84,"name":"42…Bc8","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"42…Bc8 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 91% to 0%. It allows mate in 1.","points":["White answers 43.Qg7#: checkmate.","Better was 42…Rc3 (−6.39), which attacks the bishop on f3, which is undefended."],"better":"42…Rc3 stops the mate threat Qg7#. After 43.Kh2 fxe6 44.Be4 Qxe4 45.Qxc3 Qxd5 46.f3 Qxf3 47.Qxf3 Bxf3 48.Kg1, the queens come off and Black comes out a piece up (−6.39)."}],"opening":{"name":"Sicilian Defense","eco":"B20","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 1…c5: 2 moves in all, 1 by White and 1 by Black.","White left the book with 2.h3. In 307,447 games from this position, strong players played 2.Nf3 (76%), 2.Nc3 (10%), 2.c3 (5%) or 2.d4 (4%).","The main line goes on 2.Nf3 d6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4 Nf6 5.Nc3 a6 6.Be3 e5.","From here White scored 53% in those games, with 15% drawn."]}}